[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Satisfy options parity with a home-level install, and defer the rest

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-AI-STRICTDOC-HOME-PARITY
TITLE: Satisfy options parity with a home-level install, and defer the rest
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
checks/options-doc.nix demands exact home-manager parity for an `ai.*` facet, so
the devenv-only ai.strictdoc module fails by construction. Operator's ruling:
add a home-manager module carrying `enable` and `package` only -- a user-global
install, the same shape packages/semble/modules/homeManager already has -- which
satisfies parity as a bucket item.

`grammars` is deliberately NOT mirrored at the home level, and this is a
judgement rather than an omission: managing sdoc grammars for a user with no
defined project root does not fit. The operator maintains their own
configuration in a separate repository, as most home-manager users do, and
expects grammars to be maintained through devenv inside whichever repository
owns the documents.

That makes this a real exception to the repo's config-parity rule rather than a
gap to close later, so the parity language itself needs updating to say a
surface may be intentionally home-absent when it is inherently project-scoped.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The parity rule exists so a feature is not quietly available in one place and
missing in another. A surface that is meaningless without a project root is not
that case, and forcing it into home-manager would produce an option nobody can
use correctly.
<<<
